# ADR-0033's single ingress, now serving ADR-0052's SPA as well as proxying the API.
#
# **The site address is the one thing that differs between the laptop and the VM,
# so it is the one thing read from the environment.** `PROPE_SITE_ADDRESS` defaults
# to `:80`, which is the ADR-0033 laptop target: Caddy only attempts automatic HTTPS
# for addresses that look like a domain name, so `:80` serves plain HTTP with no
# ACME involved. Set it to the domain on the VM (`prope.example.com`) and Caddy
# provisions and renews the certificate itself — the handle blocks below do not
# change, which is why this is an environment variable and not a second Caddyfile.
#
# A bare IP address is NOT a domain, so `PROPE_SITE_ADDRESS=:80` on a host reached
# by IP is the honest configuration — and it is the case that also needs
# `PROPE_COOKIE_SECURE=false`, for the reason at the bottom of this comment.
#
# **The routing separation is structural, not an ordering rule.** Caddy dispatches
# on path prefix in configuration, which is the whole reason the assets are not
# served by FastAPI: a `StaticFiles` mount plus a catch-all SPA fallback has to be
# registered *after* the API routers and after `application.mount("/v1", gateway)`,
# or the one surface `00_context.md#constraints` makes non-negotiably
# wire-compatible starts returning `index.html` to a consumer's SDK — with nothing
# in CI to catch it. ADR-0050 declined an application-maintained invariant of
# exactly that shape, in exactly that process, one pass earlier.
#
# **One origin, and everything rests on it.** The SPA and the API share this
# address, which is what keeps ADR-0029's `HttpOnly` `Secure` `SameSite=Lax` cookie
# working with **no CORS configuration and no CSRF token anywhere in Prope**
# (A-142 §8, ADR-0052). `SameSite=Lax` already blocks a cross-site POST from
# carrying the cookie; the mechanism that makes that enough is the shared origin.
# `src/prope-fe/vite.config.ts` reproduces this split as a dev-server proxy so the
# two environments have one security posture rather than two.
#
# **On plain HTTP the session cookie needs `PROPE_COOKIE_SECURE=false`.** A browser
# silently discards a `Secure` cookie sent over plain HTTP, which looks like a
# successful sign-in followed by 401s with nothing in any log. The setting defaults
# to `true`; the local demo is where it is turned off, and `docker-compose.yml`
# does so with the reason attached. A VM serving a real domain leaves it alone.
{$PROPE_SITE_ADDRESS::80} {
	log {
		output stdout
		format json
	}

	# ADR-0050's gateway sub-application. `/v1` is OpenAI's wire version and not
	# ours to bump (INT-022), and it is listed first so it reads as the surface
	# with a compatibility promise rather than as one more proxied path.
	handle /v1/* {
		reverse_proxy app:8000
	}

	# ADR-0003's management API — the only API, since ADR-0052 made the SPA its
	# first-party consumer.
	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy app:8000
	}

	# INT-030 and D-39. Unversioned by A-045 because an operator has no session and
	# a scraper has none and never will.
	handle /metrics {
		reverse_proxy app:8000
	}

	handle /healthz {
		reverse_proxy app:8000
	}

	handle /readyz {
		reverse_proxy app:8000
	}

	# Everything else is the SPA. `try_files {path} /index.html` is the client-side
	# router's fallback: `/app/prompts/prm_...` is a route React knows about and the
	# filesystem does not, so a reload of a deep link must serve the shell rather
	# than 404.
	handle {
		root * /srv
		try_files {path} /index.html
		file_server
	}
}

# The consumer surface on its own hostname — the shape `api.openai.com` uses, and
# for its reason. OPTIONAL: `PROPE_API_ADDRESS` defaults to `:8081`, a port no
# deployment publishes, so a host that ignores this block is unchanged by it.
#
# **It is a second POLICY, not a second route in.** Measured against
# `api.openai.com`: that hostname answers with `server: cloudflare` and a
# `cf-ray`, so it is fully proxied and its origin address is never published. What
# the separate name buys is a CDN policy of its own — bot protection that scores
# instead of blocking, no browser challenge, no minification — because `/api` and
# `/v1` have incompatible callers. A browser surface wants challenges; `/v1` is an
# SDK with an API key, no cookie, no referer and a user-agent like `python-httpx`,
# which is the exact signature a challenge is built to refuse.
#
# So this is served on **443, proxied, alongside the main site**, and NOT on a
# stray port. Caddy multiplexes both hostnames on 443 by SNI. A non-standard port
# is the tempting version and the wrong one: the CDN does not forward arbitrary
# ports, so reaching one means a DNS-only record, which publishes the origin
# address permanently — and since nothing in Prope checks the `Host` header, that
# address then serves `prope.site` to anybody who asks for it. That trades a
# path-scoped problem for a permanent, un-revocable one.
#
# **`/api/*` is deliberately absent, and adding it would break sign-in.** ADR-0029's
# session cookie is `SameSite=Lax` with no CSRF token anywhere, and what makes that
# safe is the SPA and the management API sharing one origin (A-142 §8, ADR-0052).
# A cookie set on `prope.site` is not sent to `api.prope.site`, so the management
# API here would need CORS, `SameSite=None` and a CSRF token — three things Prope
# does not have — to serve a surface no browser calls. The gateway has no such
# problem: INT-022 authenticates with an `Authorization: Bearer prope_key_...`
# header, which crosses origins by itself and is why OpenAI can serve its API from
# a hostname that holds no session either.
#
# **Getting the certificate through a proxying CDN.** Caddy solves ACME HTTP-01 on
# port 80, which the CDN forwards, so `PROPE_API_ADDRESS=api.prope.site` provisions
# normally. TLS-ALPN does not survive a proxy and Caddy falls back on its own. If
# the zone is set to Full (strict) and issuance is blocked for any reason, the
# other supported answer is a CDN-issued origin certificate and an explicit `tls`
# directive here.
{$PROPE_API_ADDRESS::8081} {
	log {
		output stdout
		format json
	}

	handle /v1/* {
		reverse_proxy app:8000
	}

	# Liveness only, so a consumer can separate "the gateway is down" from "my key
	# is wrong" without holding a key. `/readyz` and `/metrics` stay on the main
	# address: one names internal dependencies, the other is an operator's.
	handle /healthz {
		reverse_proxy app:8000
	}

	# Everything else — `/api/*`, the SPA, `/metrics` — refused here in
	# configuration, rather than left to whichever handler happens to sort first.
	handle {
		respond "Not found. This address serves the OpenAI-compatible gateway under /v1 only." 404
	}
}
